T*
(   3. Transmission Modes Comparison:) Tj
T*
(   - Simplex Mode:) Tj
T*
(   * Directionality: Strictly unidirectional data transfer; channel capacity is permanently) Tj
T*
(   dedicated to one direction.) Tj
T*
(   * Mechanism: Device A can only transmit; Device B can only receive.) Tj
T*
(   * Practical Computing Examples: Traditional computer keyboard to CPU motherboard interface, GPS) Tj
T*
(   receiver receiving satellite signals, CRT/LCD monitor receiving video signals from GPU.) Tj
T*
(   - Half-Duplex Mode:) Tj
T*
(   * Directionality: Bidirectional communication, but strictly alternating: transmission can occur) Tj
T*
(   in either direction, but NEVER simultaneously.) Tj
T*
(   * Mechanism: When Device A transmits, Device B must listen; line turn-around time is required) Tj
T*
(   to switch roles.) Tj
T*
(   * Practical Computing Examples: Walkie-talkie radio systems, classical legacy 10BASE2 coaxial) Tj
T*
(   Ethernet with CSMA/CD, I2C embedded microcontroller sensor bus.) Tj
T*
(   - Full-Duplex Mode:) Tj
T*
(   * Directionality: Simultaneous bidirectional communication; both parties can transmit and) Tj
T*
(   receive data concurrently.) Tj
T*
(   * Mechanism: The physical channel is divided into two separate paths \(separate wire pairs or) Tj
T*
(   distinct carrier frequencies\).) Tj
T*
(   * Practical Computing Examples: Modern switched 1000BASE-T Gigabit Ethernet, standard mobile) Tj
T*
(   cellular phone voice calls, bidirectional TCP socket connections.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
