BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Distinguish between Circuit Switching and Packet Switching. Detail the differences in resource reservation, connection establishment, bandwidth utilization, latency characteristics, and resilience to node failures.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Architectural Principles:) Tj
T*
(   - Circuit Switching: Establishes a dedicated, physical, unshared end-to-end communication path) Tj
T*
(   between caller and callee prior to data exchange \(originating in PSTN telephone networks\).) Tj
T*
(   - Packet Switching: Data is segmented into discrete independent packets containing headers) Tj
T*
(   \(with source/destination addresses\) that travel dynamically across shared communication links) Tj
T*
(   using store-and-forward routing \(the architecture of the Internet\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Detailed Comparative Analysis:) Tj
T*
(   - 1. Resource Reservation & Bandwidth Allocation:) Tj
T*
(   * Circuit Switching: Bandwidth and switch resources are dedicated exclusively for the entire) Tj
T*
(   duration of the call. If no data is transmitted \(silent pauses\), the reserved bandwidth is) Tj
T*
(   wasted \(poor efficiency for bursty computer data\).) Tj
T*
(   * Packet Switching: Bandwidth is allocated dynamically on demand using statistical) Tj
T*
(   time-division multiplexing. Multiple users share the same physical link concurrently, achieving) Tj
T*
(   maximum bandwidth utilization.) Tj
T*
(   - 2. Connection Setup Phase:) Tj
T*
(   * Circuit Switching: Requires a mandatory connection setup signaling phase before transmission) Tj
T*
(   begins, introducing initial setup delay.) Tj
T*
(   * Packet Switching: Datagram networks require zero connection setup delay; packets are) Tj
T*
(   dispatched immediately into the network.) Tj
T*
(   - 3. Latency and Jitter Characteristics:) Tj
T*
(   * Circuit Switching: Once connected, transmission delay is strictly constant; propagation delay) Tj
T*
(   is predictable; zero queuing delay; zero jitter \(ideal for legacy real-time analog voice\).) Tj
T*
(   * Packet Switching: Variable queuing delay occurs at intermediate router buffers depending on) Tj
T*
(   network congestion; introduces packet jitter and potential packet loss if buffers overflow.) Tj
T*
(   - 4. Resilience and Fault Tolerance:) Tj
T*
(   * Circuit Switching: Highly vulnerable; if an intermediate switch or trunk line fails, the) Tj
T*
(   active circuit drops immediately and must be redialed.) Tj
T*
(   * Packet Switching: Highly robust and self-healing; routers dynamically re-route subsequent) Tj
T*
(   packets around failed nodes or severed fiber links without dropping the application session.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
